1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Ask AI to write more tests?

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:04,300
You probably don’t want to do that.

3
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:08,967
In a large legacy system, tickets arrive faster than your team can close them.

4
00:00:08,967 --> 00:00:10,733
“Not enough automated tests!”

5
00:00:10,733 --> 00:00:12,067
You’re probably right.

6
00:00:12,067 --> 00:00:15,467
But AI can pile on code before it provides protection.

7
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:19,633
More complexity. More upkeep. A harder queue to clear.

8
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:25,200
Test automation requires high-level software engineering—and must protect the original intent.

9
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,467
Targeted tests can still help now.

10
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:31,100
A better use of AI here? Hands-on testing.

11
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:35,133
First, make an isolated, repeatable environment easy to set up.

12
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:38,467
Show AI your manual checks; let it repeat them.

13
00:00:38,467 --> 00:00:40,867
Investigate, confirm, fix—

14
00:00:40,867 --> 00:00:43,067
without adding test code to maintain.

15
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:47,100
Once you regain control, turn useful checks into repeatable test code:

16
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,033
no AI needed to run it.

17
00:00:49,033 --> 00:00:52,333
For new features, express your intent in tests first.

18
00:00:52,333 --> 00:00:53,900
Let them drive development.

19
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:58,200
Keep improving the suite: faster feedback, fewer redundant tests,

20
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,267
suitable local checks moved to units.

21
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:02,433
Keep essential end-to-end protection.

22
00:01:02,433 --> 00:01:05,100
Use spare AI to simplify and fix.

23
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:09,533
Less to carry. Fewer bugs to chase.
